{"text":"A popularity-smoothed first-order model, P(j|i)=[C(i,j)+αp(j)]/[C(i,·)+α], interpolates between observed next-item transitions and popularity; sparse source items can therefore behave almost entirely like popularity-only. Its advantage over popularity-only, last-item embedding models, recency-weighted item-item aggregation, or SASRec hybrids must be established experimentally on the matched splits and candidates. Recency-weighted aggregation is a stronger inexpensive control because it combines evidence from several history items without contextual interactions. Spotify’s results support testing this baseline before increasing architecture complexity. [Spotify explanation](https://research.atspotify.com/2026/10/do-sequential-recommendation-benchmarks-require-higher-order-sequence-modelling)

Recommended ablations: popularity-only; unsmoothed versus popularity- and uniform-prior smoothing; last-item versus equally weighted versus recency-weighted history; directed transitions versus unordered co-occurrence; source-independent destination counts; shuffled training histories preserving item frequencies. For the residual, compare SASRec alone, SASRec+popularity, SASRec+transition, and SASRec+both with equal validation-tuning budgets and calibrated score scales. A transition term that improves beyond SASRec+popularity, survives popularity stratification, and loses its advantage after transition destruction provides stronger evidence of transition information. Report per-category HR@10, macro average, source-transition support, and paired uncertainty on the fixed validation users; these are proposed controls, not published findings.

Spotify evaluates seven models: MC, FMC, FMC+, SeqRules, PCTM, sampled-softmax SASRec, and eSASRec, using full-catalogue NDCG@10 on Amazon Beauty/Sports/Toys and MovieLens-1M/20M under eSASRec’s protocol. Pairwise probes beat both Transformers on four datasets; eSASRec wins ML-20M. PCTM uses uniform-prior smoothing plus a separate popularity adjustment—not popularity-prior smoothing. This tests the need for joint contextual modelling; it does not establish transition residual gains over popularity or SASRec hybrids, nor directly predict RecHarness’s sampled-candidate HR@10. [Official implementations and results](https://github.com/spotify-research/sequential-capacity-probes)"}
